summary: "Using county-level variation from the rollout of the Secure Communities program between 2008-2014, we study how farms adjust to increased labor insecurity. Preliminary evidence suggests that, rather than raising native wages or employment, farms respond to immigration enforcement–induced labor shortages by shifting crop mix and increasing mechanization. We observe heterogeneity in effect size, which can be explained by baseline differences in county jail capacity and preexisting interior enforcement agreements."
